WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:09.700
All right welcome everyone to this brief
webinar on the HTTPS protocol and Some

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.730
changes coming to NCBI web services.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.200
my name is Eric Sayers i'll be talking
to you for a few minutes today about

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.130
what these changes are and what you can
expect and what you should do.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.899
i'm here with my colleague peter cooper
who will be monitoring the questions

00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:31.239
during the webinar and we'll try to get
to as many of them as we can. So we're

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:35.710
going to be talking about today are Some
upcoming changes to the way that

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.050
NCBI receives web service requests and
that primarily has to do with a

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.749
protocol called HTTPS which is
essentially secure HTTP. it's Something

00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:51.100
that has been around for Some time and
it's becoming more popular for a variety

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.670
of reaSons; we'll talk a little bit about
what Some of those are. So what we're going to

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:00.010
try and do this afternoon is talk about
why we're doing this, because we have to,

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:04.239
this is a federal mandate for all
websites maintained by the federal

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:05.229
government;

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:11.020
Some of the benefits that we get from
using HTTPS; and then what's actually

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.630
happening at NCBI in particular

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:18.490
and what would be the consequences for
you or your users or customers that are

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.770
using Software products that you're
making, etc., and perhaps most interesting

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.940
to you, what should you do

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.940
now what do you need to do to respond to
these changes

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.740
what can you expect in the future and
then Some details.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.250
So I'm going to talk very generally
about this, i'm not going to get into a

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.050
lot of technical detail.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.530
we're going to have Some additional web
pages that will be going up very Soon

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:46.600
hopefully this week that will describe
more of the detail of what you might

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.040
need to be aware of, and i'll give you of
course Some contact information at the

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.150
end if you do have additional questions,
that you can get in touch with us.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.740
So the reaSon why we're doing this is
because we were told to by the office of

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.870
management and budget.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:07.150
this is a federal office that controls a
variety of things, but does supervise the

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.340
web presence of the federal government.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:14.650
You can find out more about this
going to https.cio.gov at the top of

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:15.940
this page.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.920
and it talks to you about the HTTPS only
standard that is now being rolled out

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.570
throughout the federal government. and
this was an announcement that was made

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:32.020
actually about a year ago in june 2015
that all federal agencies must make all

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:36.970
of their current existing websites and
services available and accessible

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.540
through HTTPS only protocols

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.650
by the end of this calendar year, by
December 31st, 2016.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:51.160
So that's the challenge, that's the
mandate that we have and So that is the

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.740
reaSon why these changes are coming
about.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:57.940
and just FYI of course this means that
any other government website that you're

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.030
dealing with is going to have to do
these same changes as well, So it

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.830
certainly is not limited to NCBI or NLM or NIH or anything like that.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.300
why are we doing this, why is the
government and many many other private

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.100
enterprises switching to HTTPS?
Well for a large number of reaSons only a few

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.200
of which i've outlined here.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.150
HTTPS encrypts all of the web traffic
between the server and clients So you

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:27.640
now have an encrypted transfer of
information which gives you a heightened

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.350
privacy and security.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:35.680
So using HTTPS provides not just privacy
and security but a consistent level of

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.560
privacy and security across all federal
websites, all of the data that is

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.000
transferred during these transactions
maintains its own privacy and it

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.220
prevents various kinds of attacks. So it
protects, it prevents, for example, unknown

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.980
websites or web sites that are trying to
hack into various sites from masquerading

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.820
as a federal site. So it protects the
public from other sites that are trying

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.180
to pretend that they might be NCBI or NLM.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.550
It alSo prevents the injection of
malware into web traffic So if Someone

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:15.430
was to intercept web traffic between the
server and the client then they could,

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:21.190
under HTTP, inject code potentially into
that traffic and the user wouldn't be

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:26.020
aware of that, and that malware would
appear to have come from the trusted

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.350
site that they're trying to get
information from. This is an obvious

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.980
problem. HTTPS does eliminate many of
those kinds of concerns.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:39.160
There's a variety of other reaSons that
you might explore on the web about HTTPS

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.390
but those are a couple of the
ones that make the most impact.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.580
So what's happening at NCBI? there's a
couple of things.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:55.810
So in September, at the end of september
if you have a GET or HEAD request, and

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.090
i'll talk a little bit about what those
are in a minute,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.860
those are going to be redirected to
HTTPS if they come over HTTP.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.900
So an example there is, if you load a
PubMed article over HTTP after september

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:13.720
30th, that URL will be
automatically redirected to HTTPS and

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.430
then the same URL. So the protocol will
be switched automatically for you.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:26.170
So if you are accessing NCBI using a web
browser you don't have to do much at

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:28.000
all, this will happen automatically.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.470
You might see a variety of these
little icons as a green lock icon in

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:36.970
some browsers, other icons that indicate
to the user that you are now over a

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:40.120
secure transaction, a secure session,

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.650
HTTPS. But that's essentially all that
you probably would notice. So not much

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.790
has to be done if you're simply
accessing NCBI over the web.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:55.180
many API request also will not have
problems with this if they are simply

00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:00.190
providing get requests or head requests,
which basically means a URL.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.000
So you're using the E-utilities or
something like that and it's simply a

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.530
URL that you're posting and that will
automatically get redirected to a HTTPS.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:15.460
But if you are using HTTP POST and
you're providing the request, and these

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.940
are mainly again for api users like the
E-utilities,

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:24.160
those will be rejected after September
30th with the status 403,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.750
they simply cannot be converted to HTTPS
if they're submitted over HTTP.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:34.480
So if you have processes that are
submitting requests using HTTP POST, it's

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.000
a problem and it needs to be changed so
that those can be done over HTTPS.

00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:44.860
that's one of the major things that we
see as a potential problem for

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:49.059
developers and users out there, so that's
one to be aware of.

00:06:49.059 --> 00:06:54.549
So the consequences if you're only using a
web browser, again you probably don't

00:06:54.549 --> 00:06:58.719
need to do anything, right, or if you
have clients that are simply using or

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:01.299
accessing NCBI web pages / browsers

00:07:01.299 --> 00:07:05.439
it's not a big deal, this will
automatically redirect. But i would

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:11.199
recommend if you link to us, if you have
web pages that do have links to NCBI,

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:17.769
change them to HTTPS links in your
HTML code. It's just better practice and that

00:07:17.769 --> 00:07:22.389
way there's no redirection that needs to
happen, but it's not a requirement, if you

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:25.269
have HTTP links, they will be redirected
for you.

00:07:25.269 --> 00:07:29.800
Most web browsers again will handle
these 301 redirects elegantly and it's

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:36.219
not a problem. If you are using an API, so
these are the E-utilities or could be the

00:07:36.219 --> 00:07:44.259
blast URL API any other API that NCBI
provides, your request may fail after

00:07:44.259 --> 00:07:48.909
september 30, and in general it's going to
be hard for us to predict all the

00:07:48.909 --> 00:07:51.189
different kinds of failures that may
happen.

00:07:51.189 --> 00:07:56.739
It depends a lot on how you have set up
your API calls, but we know that if

00:07:56.739 --> 00:07:59.889
you're using HTTP POST you will have
problems.

00:07:59.889 --> 00:08:03.219
So again as I said that's the thing to
worry most about.

00:08:03.219 --> 00:08:08.919
it's possible that the mechanisms that
you're using to call the api's may not

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:14.800
handle 301 redirects properly so the
best thing to do is not even risk the

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.519
redirects but just change the protocol,
which we'll talk about in just a moment.

00:08:18.519 --> 00:08:21.579
Those are the things to be most
concerned about.

00:08:21.579 --> 00:08:25.419
So what should you do? - change the
protocol.

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:28.419
If you are accessing any NCBI
website

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:36.159
or any NCBI service or any API calls to
NCBI over HTTP, change the code protocol

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:43.209
to HTTPS. And all you have to do is just
replace HTTP with HTTPS, that's it right,

00:08:43.209 --> 00:08:46.689
all of the other parts of the URL stay
the same.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:53.790
You should know that all web services,
right, our web services online, pubmed

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:58.770
blast, all of the sequence databases, all
of entrez, all of the web pages as well

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:04.380
as all of our APIs, the E-utilities,
blast URLAPI, everything works over HTTPS

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:05.280
today.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.940
So there's no reason you can't change in
five minutes just do it now.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.720
All right, so everything works, there
shouldn't be a problem, and just switch

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:18.900
it immediately and start to use HTTPS.
Now we understand that that is not

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.400
possible for everyone. It is going to take
some time to update code bases,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.520
other kinds of software packages that
might be out there,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.030
that's why we're trying to get the word
out as quickly as we can before the

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:34.080
september 30th deadline. now you might be
asking yourself, why September

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:40.800
30 vs December 31st? The federal deadline is
is December 31st. NLM's internal

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.420
deadline is September 30th, so we're
trying to get this done early so that we

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:49.470
can fix problems before the federal
mandate takes effect.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.660
So one thing that you can do if it's not
possible to change all of your code

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:59.460
before that time, or you're not sure how
much of a problem you might be in, we

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:03.720
